[Event "Evans Gambit Accepted"]

{In the Italian, 4.b4 sacrifices a pawn to gain time for c3 and d4. Kasparov and Morphy both used it.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Aims at f7. Develops the bishop to c4, where it controls d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... Bc5 {The Giuoco Piano. Develops the bishop to c5, where it controls d4.}
4. b4 {[%cal Gb4c5] The Evans Gambit: decoys the bishop to win time.}
4... Bxb4 {Accepts. Takes the pawn on b4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5. c3 {[%cal Gc3b4] Gains a tempo on the bishop and prepares d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls d4 and eyes the bishop on b4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
5... Ba5 {The main line, keeping the bishop on the diagonal.}
 (5... Bc5 {Back to c5. Moves the bishop out of attack to c5 and controls d4.}
6. d4 {[%cal Gd4c5] Centre with tempo. The pawn on d4 eyes the bishop on c5 and the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
6... exd4 {Takes the pawn on d4; White will want to recapture.}
7. cxd4 {Recaptures; a big centre. Recaptures on d4, restoring the material balance.}
7... Bb4+ {Check. It is check, so the reply is forced. Moves the bishop out of attack to b4. It gives check.}
8. Bd2 {Blocks and offers a trade. Develops the bishop to d2, where it controls e3 and f4. Black's last move put a bishop on b4, which this now pressures.}
)
6. d4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4] The point: a big centre and fast development.}
6... exd4 {Takes the pawn on d4; White will want to recapture.}
 (6... d6 {Holds e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3f7] Hits f7. The queen goes to b3.}
7... Qd7 {Defends f7. The queen goes to d7.}
8. dxe5 {Opens lines. Takes the pawn on e5; Black will want to recapture.}
8... dxe5 {Recaptures on e5, restoring the material balance.}
9. O-O {Castles; Black's king is stuck in the centre for now.}
9... Bb6 {Re-routes the bishop. Regroups the bishop to b6 and controls d4.}
10. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Harasses the bishop. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on a4 takes b5 away from the opponent.}
)
7. O-O {Castles, with a lead in development. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... Nge7 {Supports a quick ...d5. Develops the knight to e7, where it controls d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8. cxd4 {Restores the centre. Takes the pawn on d4; Black will want to recapture.}
8... d5 {Returns the pawn to free the game. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 eyes the bishop on c4 and the pawn on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
9. exd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
9... Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.} *

[Event "Evans Gambit Declined & Lasker"]

{Black can decline with 4...Bb6 or return the pawn with 5...Be7.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bc4 {Italian. Develops the bishop to c4, where it controls d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... Bc5 {Piano. Develops the bishop to c5, where it controls d4.}
4. b4 {Evans Gambit. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on b4 takes a5 and c5 away from the opponent. It attacks the bishop on c5, which is undefended.}
4... Bb6 {Declines. Moves the bishop out of attack to b6 and controls d4.}
 (4... Bxb4 {Accepts. Takes the pawn on b4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5. c3 {Gains time. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls d4 and eyes the bishop on b4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
5... Be7 {The safe retreat. The bishop goes to e7.}
6. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
6... Na5 {Hits the c4-bishop. The knight goes to a5. It attacks the bishop on c4, which is undefended.}
7. Nxe5 {[%cal Ge5f7] White takes e5 with threats on f7. Takes the pawn on e5, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
7... Nxc4 {Takes the bishop. Takes the bishop on c4; White will want to recapture.}
8. Nxc4 {Recaptures on c4, restoring the material balance.}
8... d5 {Black returns the pawn to develop. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 eyes the knight on c4 and the pawn on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
9. exd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
9... Qxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
10. Ne3 {[%cal Ge3d5] Hits the queen. Moves the knight out of attack to e3 and eyes the queen on d5. That queen is currently undefended.}
)
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Gains queenside space and threatens a5. The pawn on a4 takes b5 away from the opponent.}
5... a6 {Makes room for the bishop on a7. The pawn on a6 controls b5.}
6. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.} *
